    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.ada_star_search - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.ada_star_search.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.ada_star_search()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find path using ADA\\* (Anytime Dynamic A\\*) search algorithm.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.ada_star_search()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find path using ADA\\* (Anytime Dynamic A\\*) search algorithm.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.ada_star_search()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Find path using ADA\\* (Anytime Dynamic A\\*) search algorithm.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind path using ADA* (Anytime Dynamic A*) search algorithm.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L60124-L60388){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.ada_star_search(\n    source,\n    target,\n    epsilon=1.0,\n    weight_attr=None,\n    heuristic=None,\n    reuse_planner=False,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![ADA* search](../assets/graphs/ada-star-search.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A directed weighted graph with three routes from A to F, costing 4, 6, and 5. With epsilon 1.0 ADA* behaves like A* and returns the optimal path A, B, C, F, drawn in the accent color. The card gives its cost, 1 + 2 + 1 = 4.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nADA* is an anytime optimal pathfinding algorithm that can return suboptimal paths quickly\nand iteratively improve them. The epsilon parameter controls the trade-off between solution\nquality and speed.\n\nThe search finds the path from `source=` to `target=` with the lowest total edge weight.\nIt is guided by an optional heuristic, an estimate of the remaining distance, which lets\nit skip parts of the graph that are clearly off course. Multiplying the heuristic by\n`epsilon=` makes the search greedier: it settles on a path sooner, and that path is\nguaranteed to cost at most `epsilon` times the optimum. Lowering `epsilon=` on later\ncalls (with `reuse_planner=True`) refines the same search rather than starting over.\n\nThis is useful for:\n\n- route planning on spatial graphs (road networks, grids, game maps) where a geometric\n  distance makes a good heuristic\n- time-constrained settings where a good-enough path now is better than the best path\n  later\n- interactive tools that show a quick answer first and then improve it\n\nSome details to be aware of:\n\n- edge weights come from each edge's weight field (as set with `add_edge(weight=...)`),\n  or from the edge attribute named by `weight_attr=`, with `1.0` where an edge has no\n  value. The graph does not need to be created with `weighted=True`\n- in directed graphs the path follows edge directions; undirected edges can be used\n  both ways. Weights should be non-negative\n- without a heuristic the search is equivalent to Dijkstra's algorithm and `epsilon=` has\n  no effect\n- the search runs *backward* from `target=`, so the heuristic is called as\n  `heuristic(node_id, source_id)` and must estimate the distance between a node and the\n  *source*\n- each expansion scans the edge list, so the method is best suited to small and\n  medium-sized graphs; for plain shortest paths on large graphs use `shortest_path()`\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Starting node, given as an integer node ID or a string label. An unknown label\n    raises a `ValueError`.\n\n<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Destination node, given as an integer node ID or a string label. An unknown label\n    raises a `ValueError`.\n\n<code>[epsilon]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[1.0]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Suboptimality bound; should be at least `1.0`. Defaults to `1.0`, which finds an\n    optimal path (as plain A*). With `epsilon > 1.0`, the heuristic is inflated by this\n    factor: the search explores fewer nodes and the returned path costs at most `epsilon`\n    times the optimum. Has no effect when `heuristic=` is `None`.\n\n<code>[weight_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the edge attribute to read weights from, in place of the weight field. Edges\n    without a numeric value under it cost `1.0`. If `None` (default), each edge's weight\n    field is used (`1.0` where missing).\n\n<code>[heuristic]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[Callable[[Union[int, str], Union[int, str]], float]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Function `h(node_id, source_id) -> float` estimating the path cost between a node and\n    the source node. Both arguments are always integer node IDs (use\n    `get_label_from_id()` or `get_node()` inside the function to look up labels or\n    attributes). For an optimal result with `epsilon=1.0` it must be admissible, never\n    overestimating the true cost. If the function raises an exception or returns a\n    non-numeric value, `0.0` is used silently. If `None` (default), a zero heuristic is\n    used, which is equivalent to Dijkstra's algorithm.\n\n<code>[reuse_planner]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, keep the planner (stored on the graph) so that a later call with the same\n    `source=`, `target=`, and `weight_attr=` continues the existing search instead of\n    starting a new one. This is how you refine a quick result: call first with a large\n    `epsilon=`, then again with smaller values. The match is on the values exactly as\n    passed (a label and the equivalent ID count as different), the `heuristic=` of the\n    original call is kept, and only one planner is stored at a time. If `False`\n    (default), every call builds a fresh planner.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Path from source to target as list of node IDs, starting with the source and ending\n    with the target.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If source or target nodes don't exist, or if no path exists.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nAnytime Dynamic A* (Likhachev et al., 2005) sits between A* and D* Lite: it can\nreturn a first, provably $\\epsilon$-suboptimal path very quickly, then refine that\nsame search when $\\epsilon$ is lowered on a subsequent call. Setting `epsilon=1.0`\nrecovers ordinary A* optimality; larger $\\epsilon$ inflates the heuristic so the\nsearch prunes more aggressively, trading path length for speed. The underlying\nsearch runs backward from `target=` to `source=`, which is what lets it reuse work\nwhen the goal is fixed but the source or graph weights change. Passing\n`reuse_planner=True` keeps the planner alive between calls (stashed on\n`graph._ada_star_planner`) and is the usual pattern for iterative deepening:\nwarm-start at a large $\\epsilon$, then feed a shrinking sequence. A call with a\ndifferent source or target pair replaces the stored planner with a new one.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~astar_path`) \n\n:   A* without the inflated heuristic or the stored planner.\n\n[](`~alt_heuristic`) \n\n:   An admissible heuristic built from landmarks.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nFrom A to D there are three routes: A-B-D (cost `1 + 5 = 6`), A-C-D (cost `4 + 1 = 5`),\nand A-B-C-D (cost `1 + 2 + 1 = 4`). Without a heuristic, the search finds the cheapest\nroute, which is also the longest in hops:\n\n::: {#95cefd21 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = (\n    ec.Graph(directed=True)\n    .add_nodes(\n        [\n            (\"A\", {\"x\": 0.0, \"y\": 0.0}),\n            (\"B\", {\"x\": 1.0, \"y\": 0.0}),\n            (\"C\", {\"x\": 1.0, \"y\": 2.0}),\n            (\"D\", {\"x\": 2.0, \"y\": 2.0}),\n        ]\n    )\n    .add_edges(\n        [\n            (\"A\", \"B\", 1.0),\n            (\"A\", \"C\", 4.0),\n            (\"B\", \"C\", 2.0),\n            (\"C\", \"D\", 1.0),\n            (\"B\", \"D\", 5.0),\n        ]\n    )\n)\n\npath = graph.ada_star_search(\"A\", \"D\")\n[graph.get_label_from_id(nid) for nid in path]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['A', 'B', 'C', 'D']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe nodes above carry `x` and `y` coordinates, and the straight-line distance between two\nnodes never exceeds the cost of the route between them, so it is an admissible heuristic.\nThe function receives node IDs and looks up the coordinates with `get_node()`. With\n`epsilon=1.0` the result is still optimal; with `epsilon=5.0` the inflated heuristic makes\nthe search settle early on the more expensive A-B-D route (cost `6`, within the bound\nof `5 * 4`):\n\n::: {#75a77b77 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndef euclidean(node_id, source_id):\n    a = graph.get_node(node_id).attributes\n    b = graph.get_node(source_id).attributes\n    return ((a[\"x\"] - b[\"x\"]) ** 2 + (a[\"y\"] - b[\"y\"]) ** 2) ** 0.5\n\noptimal = graph.ada_star_search(\n    \"A\", \"D\", heuristic=euclidean, epsilon=1.0\n)\ngreedy = graph.ada_star_search(\n    \"A\", \"D\", heuristic=euclidean, epsilon=5.0\n)\n\n(\n    [graph.get_label_from_id(nid) for nid in optimal],\n    [graph.get_label_from_id(nid) for nid in greedy],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'], ['A', 'B', 'D'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWith `reuse_planner=True`, a quick, greedy first answer can be refined by calling again\nwith a smaller `epsilon=`. The second call continues the stored search and reaches the\noptimal route:\n\n::: {#7e952546 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfirst = graph.ada_star_search(\n    \"A\", \"D\", heuristic=euclidean, epsilon=5.0, reuse_planner=True\n)\nrefined = graph.ada_star_search(\"A\", \"D\", epsilon=1.0, reuse_planner=True)\n\n(\n    [graph.get_label_from_id(nid) for nid in first],\n    [graph.get_label_from_id(nid) for nid in refined],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(['A', 'B', 'D'], ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWeights can also come from any other numeric edge attribute through `weight_attr=`.\nHere the costs are stored under `cost`; without `weight_attr=\"cost\"` every edge would\ncost `1.0` and the two-hop route A-B-D would win:\n\n::: {#c1030257 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncosts = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [\n        (\"A\", \"B\", {\"cost\": 1.0}),\n        (\"A\", \"C\", {\"cost\": 4.0}),\n        (\"B\", \"C\", {\"cost\": 2.0}),\n        (\"C\", \"D\", {\"cost\": 1.0}),\n        (\"B\", \"D\", {\"cost\": 5.0}),\n    ],\n    directed=True,\n)\n\nlabel = costs.get_label_from_id\n(\n    [label(nid) for nid in costs.ada_star_search(\"A\", \"D\")],\n    [\n        label(nid)\n        for nid in costs.ada_star_search(\"A\", \"D\", weight_attr=\"cost\")\n    ],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n(['A', 'B', 'D'], ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A quick plan on the metro\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network, with each stretch of track weighted by its\ntravel time in minutes. No stretch takes fewer minutes than its straight-line length on\nthe map of station positions, so the straight-line distance is admissible. Even with the\nheuristic inflated threefold, the quick plan from Docks to Northpark is the fastest route:\n\n::: {#39caf5a6 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport math\n\nmetro = ec.metro()\nnodes = metro.get_node_df()\npos = {n: tuple(p) for n, p in zip(nodes[\"id\"], nodes[\"pos\"])}\n\ndef straight_line(a, b):\n    return math.dist(pos[a], pos[b])\n\nroute = metro.ada_star_search(\n    \"Docks\", \"Northpark\", heuristic=straight_line, epsilon=3.0\n)\n[metro.get_label_from_id(n) for n in route]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```\n['Docks',\n 'Harbor',\n 'Riverside',\n 'Central',\n 'Museum',\n 'University',\n 'Northpark']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#2a5b1d99 .cell execution_count=6}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro.plot(layout=\"pos\", highlight=route, label_by=\"label\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=6}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"3.9 14.0 688.8 492.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"3